Z||000000||maryjane||05-06-2001||10:05 PM||||My 84 4 cyl has a history of leaking valve cover gaskets. The shop that replaced it told me IO had some blowby but when I opened the oil fill cap, it didn't seem too bad, more vacumn than blow. When I went to change the valve cover gasket today,2 years after the last one, I was dismayed more than a little to find hard crud built up over the outside top lip of of the cyl hd. You could hardley see the holes for the pushrods and could NOT see the 4 holes where the oil drains back down. I have changed the oil and filter fanaticaly so I was surprised. What really upset me was that sitting on top of this mess was some of the little metal rings that come in the boltholes on some cover gaskets. Since the gasket was still on the cover and all the rings were intact, the ones on top of the hardened sludge had to have been left by the shop that did the last gasket. That meant that all this mess had to have been there when he did the work and neglected to tell me. I KNOW the fact ythat it is there means a major rebuild, but I can't manage it right now.I chipped it off a little at a time, vacumning it up as I went,but some of it found it's way down anyway, especially when I unclogged the drainback holes.Ruined Jane's hoover too. I was real happy with the job when I finished till I got about 8 miles down the road to get new oil & filter and the oil/temp light comes on. I can let sit and it is good for a couple miles low speed to get me home. Now I 'm facedwith what to do with it. Try oil/filter change? Leave plug out and pour something in to rinse out pan,I'm assuming dirt is clogging the pump screen. Can you drop the pan enough to clean it maybe a new pump too? Sorry this is so long, I'm po folks livin in a rich folks world, Can't swing a rebuild right now. It is a daily driver, my better half's, everytime she takes mine it comes back with somethin wrong with it. Helllllp!!! ||||reg||8
Z||000001||theogre||05-06-2001||11:04 PM||||Well..... It's a pain in the ass but yes you can get the pan off actually. You'll likely have to support the motor from above to do it.<P>Sounds like you may have crapped up the pickup screen.<P><BR>Your big problem may be that you didn't shut it off and tow it home. If that is an original 84 motor, they are even more crabby than normal about being run low on oil pressure. Hopefully there is no major damage.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fierowrecker||05-07-2001||06:38 PM||||Time to bite the bullet and rebuild, or replace it...<BR>Perhaps you can find a low milage 2.5L in the yards down there!<BR>(That was a poor joke, I know!)<BR>That crud is from the blowby...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
